  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nAs of 12:30 pm CDT, temperatures around the area are in the 80s\nto low 90s with winds out of the south to southwest. Winds will\nmove around to the northwest from west to east across the \nforecast area this evening, becoming easterly by sunrise Friday \nmorning. While the threat has shifted north, there is still a \nsmall portion of Roberts and Traverse counties in a Marginal \nRisk (level 1 out of 5) for severe storms this evening. The vast\nmajority of ensembles and CAMs have the storms staying to our \nnorth and east, however, so confidence is low that we will \nactually see severe storms. If a storm does happen to clip our \narea, hazards will be 60 mph winds and hail up to 1 inch. Some \nfog is also expected early Friday morning with visibilities \ndropping to around 2 miles in portions of northeast SD and west \ncentral MN.\n\nFriday into the weekend, an upper level ridge develops with its axis \nover the forecast area. Several shortwaves will move along the ridge \nbringing some chances for storms areas around and east of the James \nRiver where moisture levels are higher. Deterministic models really \ndiffer on the amount of instability in this area, ranging from about \n1000 J/kg to over 2000 J/kg Saturday evening. However, most agree \nthat bulk shear is pretty marginal at less than 30 kts. Sunday \nafternoon and evening, bulk shear is about the same but more of the \ndeterministic models show MLCAPE closer to 2000 J/kg. Due to the low \nshear, no severe weather is expected at this time. A low pressure \nsystem out of Montana late Sunday into Monday will bring more \nwidespread storm chances to the area, drawing moisture up from an \nopen Gulf. PWATs Monday afternoon are forecast to be in the 1.5 to \n1.75\" range or the 99.5 NAEFS percentiles. This could result in \nheavy rainfall with the storms. These higher amounts remain in place \nthrough the overnight hours and into Tuesday afternoon. Confidence \nis low on severe potential at this time, but keep an eye on the SPC \noutlooks once this time period moves into the Days 1-3 range.\n\nThe ridge mentioned previously will bring warmer air into the region \npushing temperatures 15-20 degrees above average Friday and \nSaturday. Winds across far western portions of north central SD and \nsouth central SD can expect wind gusts of 30-35 mph Friday \nafternoon, but otherwise, winds will be unremarkable until Monday. \nWith the incoming low pressure, wind gusts Monday afternoon look to \nbe in the 30 to 35 mph range between the Missouri River and Prairie \nCoteau of eastern SD.",
